				<title>(no title)</title>
				<link>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-625542/history-of-the-universe:part-one#post-1741146</link>
				<description></description>
				<pubDate>Fri, 29 Mar 2013 17:51:27 +0000</pubDate>
				<wikidot:authorName>VAElynx</wikidot:authorName>				<wikidot:authorUserId>914331</wikidot:authorUserId>				<content:encoded>
					<![CDATA[
						 <p>Few nitpicks</p> <p>Ad Administrator</p> <blockquote> <p>Although currently seen as a lackadaisical manager, he must still be given credit for his work in establishing the wiki's basis.</p> </blockquote> <p>Currently seen by who? Seriously, this is a filler sentence that should be cut, the stuff beyond it fully tells what he did.</p> <blockquote> <p>SCP-001 was another point, if a comparatively minor one. The spot of SCP-001 was left open by the Administrator, to see what would happen. Masipag locked the page up, and in the debate that followed claimed that she didn't think we ought to have any 001 proposals at all. Today we have ten SCP-001 proposals, so she was correct at that time. If there'd been only one 001 proposal, we would've never seen some of the wiki's better pieces.</p> </blockquote> <p>This doesn't make sense. If someone thought there shouldn't be any 001 proposals, they are not proven correct by us having ten, unless they're so awful we'd be better without any (they aren't)</p> <blockquote> <p>There are also some designations which are no longer in use. For example, on the original /x/ SCP Series, there was the Thaumiel designation, which was basically the &quot;Anti-Keter&quot; article series.</p> </blockquote> <p>Sorry but 'Anti-Keter' is about as enlightening as 'Thaumiel'</p> 
				 	]]>
				</content:encoded>							</item>
